    {
      "id": "wire",
      "title": "Wire and trace sizes",
      "summary": "Resistance and current by cable size, voltage drop over a run, and fusing",
      "lesson": "power",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Drop = current × resistance of the whole loop, out and back. Aim for 3% or less on power runs. Fuse below the cable's rating, above the load's current, as close to the battery as you can."
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Cross-section",
            "Nearest AWG",
            "Ohms per metre",
            "Typical rating"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0.5 mm²",
              "20",
              "0.034 Ω",
              "3 A"
            ],
            [
              "1.5 mm²",
              "16",
              "0.011 Ω",
              "13 A"
            ],
            [
              "2.5 mm²",
              "14",
              "0.0069 Ω",
              "20 A"
            ],
            [
              "4 mm²",
              "12",
              "0.0043 Ω",
              "25 A"
            ],
            [
              "6 mm²",
              "10",
              "0.0029 Ω",
              "32 A"
            ],
            [
              "10 mm²",
              "8",
              "0.0017 Ω",
              "45 A"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Ratings depend on insulation, bundling and air temperature; take the cable maker's figure over this table. Trace widths on a board follow the same rule: a 1 oz copper trace 0.25 mm wide carries well under an amp."
        },
        {
          "kind": "figure",
          "figure": "long-wire",
          "caption": "20 m of cable to a 60 W pump: pick the size, or raise the voltage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "signals",
      "title": "Signals and the scope",
      "summary": "Frequency and period, peak and RMS, and setting a scope",
      "lesson": "signals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Quantity",
            "Means",
            "Formula"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Period",
              "Time for one cycle",
              "T = 1 ÷ f"
            ],
            [
              "Frequency",
              "Cycles a second, in hertz",
              "f = 1 ÷ T"
            ],
            [
              "Amplitude",
              "Middle to peak",
              "A"
            ],
            [
              "Peak to peak",
              "Bottom to top",
              "2 × A"
            ],
            [
              "RMS of a sine",
              "The steady volts that heat as much",
              "A × 0.707"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Signal",
            "Frequency",
            "Size"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Hearing",
              "20 Hz to 20 kHz",
              "–"
            ],
            [
              "Guitar, low E string",
              "82 Hz",
              "a few hundred mV from a pickup"
            ],
            [
              "Mains",
              "50 or 60 Hz",
              "230 V or 120 V RMS"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "On a scope, set the volts a square so the wave fills most of the screen’s height, and the time a square so two or three cycles fit across. Check the steady voltages with a meter before looking at the signal."
        },
        {
          "kind": "terms",
          "items": [
            {
              "term": "Bias",
              "means": "The steady voltage a signal rides on; half the supply gives the most headroom."
            },
            {
              "term": "Clipping",
              "means": "A wave cut flat where a stage runs out of room."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "capacitors",
      "title": "Capacitors and time",
      "summary": "RC time, filters and their corners, decoupling, and common types",
      "lesson": "capacitors",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "To find",
            "Formula",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Time constant",
              "τ = R × C",
              "10 kΩ × 100 µF = 1 s"
            ],
            [
              "Charge after t",
              "V × (1 − e^(−t/τ))",
              "63% at τ, 86% at 2τ, 99% at 5τ"
            ],
            [
              "Corner frequency",
              "f = 1 ÷ (2π R C)",
              "10 kΩ, 10 nF: 1.6 kHz"
            ],
            [
              "Reactance",
              "X = 1 ÷ (2π f C)",
              "100 nF at 1 kHz: 1.6 kΩ"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Type",
            "Values",
            "Used for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Ceramic",
              "1 pF to 10 µF",
              "Decoupling (100 nF by every chip), filters"
            ],
            [
              "Film",
              "1 nF to 10 µF",
              "Audio paths and tone controls"
            ],
            [
              "Electrolytic",
              "1 µF and up",
              "Reservoirs and big coupling caps; has a + and −"
            ],
            [
              "Tantalum",
              "0.1 to 100 µF",
              "Small boards; fails short if reversed"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Capacitor markings: “104” is 10 and four noughts in picofarads, 100 nF; “473” is 47 nF. Choose a voltage rating well above the voltage across the part."
        },
        {
          "kind": "terms",
          "items": [
            {
              "term": "Low-pass",
              "means": "R in the path, C to ground: passes below the corner."
            },
            {
              "term": "High-pass",
              "means": "C in the path, R to ground: passes above the corner."
            },
            {
              "term": "Kickback",
              "means": "The spike a coil makes when its current is cut. Lesson 8 adds the diode that catches it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "semiconductors",
      "title": "Diodes and transistors",
      "summary": "Forward voltages, LED resistors, and transistors as switches",
      "lesson": "diodes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Diode",
            "Forward voltage",
            "Used for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Silicon (1N4148, 1N400x)",
              "0.6–0.7 V",
              "Signals, rectifiers, protection, clipping"
            ],
            [
              "Germanium (1N34A)",
              "0.2–0.3 V",
              "Soft clipping, old radios"
            ],
            [
              "Schottky (1N5817, BAT46)",
              "0.2–0.4 V",
              "Low-loss protection, solar blocking"
            ],
            [
              "Red LED",
              "1.8–2 V",
              "Indicators, loud clipping"
            ],
            [
              "Green LED",
              "2–2.2 V",
              "Indicators"
            ],
            [
              "Blue or white LED",
              "2.8–3.2 V",
              "Indicators, lighting"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Transistor",
            "Kind",
            "Good for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "2N3904, BC547",
              "NPN, small",
              "Switching up to 100 mA, small amplifiers"
            ],
            [
              "2N3906, BC557",
              "PNP, small",
              "High-side switching, the other half of a pair"
            ],
            [
              "2N7000",
              "N MOSFET, small",
              "Logic switching up to 200 mA"
            ],
            [
              "IRLZ44N",
              "N MOSFET, logic-level",
              "Motors and lights up to tens of amps from 5 V"
            ],
            [
              "IRF540",
              "N MOSFET, standard",
              "Big loads, with a 10 V gate drive"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "terms",
          "items": [
            {
              "term": "LED resistor",
              "means": "R = (supply − LED voltage) ÷ current. 2 to 20 mA."
            },
            {
              "term": "Ripple",
              "means": "≈ load current ÷ (frequency × reservoir capacitance); a bridge doubles the frequency."
            },
            {
              "term": "Temperature",
              "means": "A diode’s forward voltage falls about 2 mV for each degree warmer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "terms",
          "items": [
            {
              "term": "Base resistor",
              "means": "R = (control − 0.7 V) ÷ (load current ÷ 10)."
            },
            {
              "term": "Flyback diode",
              "means": "Across every switched coil, cathode to the supply."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "switching",
      "title": "Switching loads",
      "summary": "Low-side and high-side switches, relays, and what each part needs",
      "lesson": "switches",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Switch",
            "Where it goes",
            "Drive"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "NPN transistor",
              "Low side, load to ground",
              "Base current a tenth of the load’s, through a resistor"
            ],
            [
              "N MOSFET",
              "Low side",
              "Gate well above threshold; 10–100 kΩ gate to source"
            ],
            [
              "PNP transistor or P MOSFET",
              "High side, supply to load",
              "Pull the base or gate down to turn on"
            ],
            [
              "Relay",
              "Either; contacts are a separate circuit",
              "A transistor for the coil, and a flyback diode"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Check the switch’s power: fully on, a transistor loses about 0.1 V × current and a MOSFET I² × its on-resistance. Half on, it loses far more."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "multimeter",
      "title": "Using a multimeter",
      "summary": "Volts across, amps through, and what each reading tells you",
      "lesson": "what-flows",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Volts are measured **across**: one probe on each side of the part, circuit left alone. Amps are measured **through**: break the loop and put the meter in the gap."
        },
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "items": [
            "Volts: dial to V (DC is the straight-line symbol), black lead in COM, red in V.",
            "Touch black to ground or one side of the part, red to the other. A minus sign means red is lower than black.",
            "Amps: dial to A or mA, black in COM, red moved to the A or mA socket.",
            "Break the loop (lift a wire, open a link) and put the probes in the gap, so the current goes through the meter.",
            "Move the red lead back to V when you're done. A meter left in amps and put across a battery shorts it and blows its fuse."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "figure",
          "figure": "meter",
          "caption": "Volts across, amps through. Tap a wire to place a probe or cut the loop."
        }
      ]
    },
